    "result_text": "I added the three password reset helpers to `CTFd/utils/security/email.py`. A human still needs to confirm one approval line in the pull request. I haven't run the code or any tests.\n\n- **`generate_reset_password_token(addr, timeout=1800)`:** creates a random 256-bit token and stores the email in the cache under a `reset_password_` key. The token expires after 30 minutes by default.\n- **`verify_reset_password_token(nonce)`:** returns the email for a token. It raises `UserResetPasswordTokenInvalidException` if the token is unknown or expired.\n- **`remove_reset_password_token(nonce)`:** deletes the token so it can't be used again.\n\n`CTFd/auth.py` and `CTFd/utils/initialization/__init__.py` already import `verify_reset_password_token`, and `auth.py` also imports `remove_reset_password_token`. Neither function existed in this file before, so those imports were broken until now.\n\nThe helpers follow the catalog pattern `security.email_confirm_token@v1`, which this file already uses for the email-confirmation helpers. I used a separate `reset_password_` key prefix so a confirmation token can't be used to reset a password.\n\n`check_code` flagged one real deviation. The pattern raises `UserConfirmTokenInvalidException`, but the reset flow needs `UserResetPasswordTokenInvalidException`, because `auth.py` catches that one. I added two `# pcat-allow` lines (`PC100`, `PC102`, hash `#514686d7`) above `verify_reset_password_token`. They approve nothing until a human confirms them in the pull request.\n\nThe checker also reported two low-severity differences, the changed key prefix in `generate_reset_password_token` and `remove_reset_password_token`, and I left those as they are."
